. The Short Answer
The widgets we built are modern ttk (Themed Tk) at the core, styled to match your dark/light themes, using raw tk only for the 2 internal mechanisms that Tkinter doesn't provide in ttk (the floating popup window and the scrollable canvas).

2. Why Tutorials Warn About tk. vs ttk.
In raw Tkinter, there are two generations of widgets:

Feature	Classic tk. (Old)	Modern ttk. (Themed)
Origin	1990s (Windows 95 / Unix Motif)	Tk 8.5+ (Modern OS-native styling)
Appearance	Gray, beveled, blocky, 3D borders	Flat, clean, modern, native OS styles
Styling	Per-widget hardcoded args (bg="red", relief="raised")	Clean style engine (ttk.Style) with hover states, active states, etc.
Drop-in replacements	tk.Button
tk.Entry
tk.Label
tk.Checkbutton	ttk.Button
ttk.Entry
ttk.Label
ttk.Checkbutton
Widgets exclusive to ttk	(None)	ttk.Notebook (Tabs), ttk.Treeview (Spreadsheet tables), ttk.Progressbar, ttk.Combobox
If you use tk.Button or tk.Entry today, your app will literally look like it was made in 1995. That's why tutorials strongly urge you to use from tkinter import ttk.

3. How We Built MultiSelectDropdown & quick_tk
Because we wanted your app to look modern (Dark Obsidian theme, smooth flat colors, clean hover feedback), everything you interact with uses ttk:

The dropdown bar: Uses ttk.Entry and ttk.Button (styled with flat borders and hover transitions).
The search box: Uses ttk.Entry so typing and placeholder text match modern Windows inputs.
The checkboxes inside the list: Every single option is a ttk.Checkbutton (style="TCheckbutton").
The scrollbar: Uses ttk.Scrollbar with modern slim thumbs.
The quick action buttons ([All], [None]): Use ttk.Button.
Where raw tk was used (and why):
Tkinter does not have ttk versions for two specific things:

tk.Toplevel: In Tkinter, all popup windows and dialogs must inherit from tk.Toplevel (there is no ttk.Toplevel).
tk.Canvas: In Tkinter, there is no built-in scrollable frame widget. The standard Python GUI pattern to make a scrollable list of checkboxes is to place a frame inside a tk.Canvas.
To keep it seamless, our Theme system injects matching background hex colors (bg=theme["surface_bg"]) into those tk primitives, so they blend invisibly with the modern ttk widgets.

4. Golden Rule for Your Studies
As you go through your tutorial, keep this simple rule of thumb:

Always default to ttk.* for anything visual:
python


from tkinter import ttk
btn = ttk.Button(...)
entry = ttk.Entry(...)
table = ttk.Treeview(...)
Only use raw tk.* when you need:
tk.Tk() (the main window)
tk.Toplevel() (popups / secondary windows)
tk.Canvas (custom 2D graphics or scrolling areas)
tk.Text (multi-line rich text editor)
tk.StringVar(), tk.BooleanVar(), tk.IntVar() (data binding variables)
That's why quick_tk was created—it wraps all of that boilerplate so you get modern ttk styling and clean inputs without having to manually configure ttk.Style() dictionaries every time!

